{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/daily-life-across-europe-japan-and-argentina/audio/main-95dea27cd109.mp3","durationSec":350.21,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":6.76,"text":"Europe contains many countries, languages, and cultural traditions, so it is not useful to imagine one “European day.”"},{"start":7.42,"end":11.32,"text":"A routine in Madrid may be different from one in Amsterdam or Berlin."},{"start":12.2,"end":16.98,"text":"We will compare those named examples with Japan and Argentina through four daily clocks."},{"start":17.84,"end":20.7,"text":"The movement clock is how people travel and behave on the way."},{"start":21.2,"end":23.12,"text":"The meal clock is when and how they eat."},{"start":23.74,"end":26.2,"text":"The contact clock is how they greet and share space."},{"start":26.88,"end":30.66,"text":"The shared-time clock is how schools, shops, and weekends are organized."},{"start":31.6,"end":33.24,"text":"These clocks are not exact times."},{"start":33.68,"end":39.16,"text":"They are a simple way to notice how local transport, rules, and social expectations shape an ordinary day."},{"start":39.86,"end":42.38,"text":"They describe common patterns, not every individual."},{"start":43.06,"end":45.48,"text":"A morning journey shows how the movement clock works."},{"start":46.22,"end":50.72,"text":"In Japan’s major cities, public transport carries millions of people each day."},{"start":50.72,"end":56.9,"text":"Official guidance asks passengers to line up, keep conversations quiet, and avoid phone calls on trains."},{"start":56.9,"end":66.12,"text":"In the Netherlands, bicycles are an everyday form of transport: the national government says that 27 percent of all journeys are made by bicycle."},{"start":67.04,"end":68.34,"text":"Buenos Aires has another pattern."},{"start":68.88,"end":76.38,"text":"People use buses called colectivos and an underground system called the subte, and both use a rechargeable SUBE card."},{"start":77.1,"end":78.96,"text":"City buses operate all day and night."},{"start":79.7,"end":90.56,"text":"A commuter in Japan, a cyclist in the Netherlands, and a bus passenger in Buenos Aires all need to reach work or school, but their streets and transport systems produce different habits."},{"start":91.22,"end":95.06,"text":"The meal clock can make the same hour feel completely different."},{"start":95.46,"end":101.2,"text":"Official European employment guidance says that a normal lunch time in Spain is from 1:30 p.m."},{"start":101.24,"end":102.72,"text":"to 3:30 p.m.,"},{"start":102.72,"end":105.14,"text":"while dinner is commonly between 9 p.m."},{"start":105.3,"end":106.4,"text":"and 11 p.m."},{"start":107.26,"end":118.78,"text":"In Buenos Aires, the traditional merienda is a late-afternoon snack, often coffee with cake or a pastry, and many people plan dinner and social activities later in the evening."},{"start":118.78,"end":122.12,"text":"Japan offers a different kind of convenience."},{"start":123.32,"end":131.7,"text":"Bento boxes are a large part of daily life and are sold in specialist shops, supermarkets, and convenience stores for people who need a prepared meal."},{"start":132.58,"end":133.86,"text":"At 2 p.m.,"},{"start":133.86,"end":139.32,"text":"one person may be sitting down to lunch in Madrid while another is buying a quick bento in Tokyo."},{"start":140.32,"end":143.92,"text":"Daily schedules change when people meet, rest, and eat together."},{"start":144.9,"end":146.72,"text":"Greetings reveal the contact clock."},{"start":147.18,"end":154.18,"text":"In everyday Japanese interactions, body contact is less common, and a polite bow is often an appropriate greeting."},{"start":155.48,"end":162.72,"text":"Official European guidance says that a kiss on each cheek or a hug is common in informal social relations in Spain."},{"start":162.72,"end":170.34,"text":"Buenos Aires’s official visitor guide describes one kiss on the cheek as a common greeting, even between colleagues."},{"start":171.12,"end":175,"text":"The important skill is not memorizing one national rule."},{"start":175.78,"end":177.86,"text":"It is reading the relationship and the setting."},{"start":177.86,"end":183.92,"text":"A close friend, a new colleague, and a customer may not receive the same greeting."},{"start":185.06,"end":193.52,"text":"Someone who expects a bow may find a kiss surprisingly close, while someone who expects a kiss may read greater distance as coldness."},{"start":194.76,"end":197.38,"text":"School routines shape life beyond the classroom."},{"start":198.24,"end":204.24,"text":"Japan’s education ministry shows school lunch and cleaning as normal parts of elementary school life."},{"start":204.58,"end":209.36,"text":"After lunch, students in the ministry’s guide clean places such as their classroom together."},{"start":210.56,"end":213.42,"text":"Buenos Aires public primary schools offer several schedules."},{"start":214.12,"end":216.52,"text":"A simple school day may run from 8 a.m."},{"start":216.62,"end":218.4,"text":"to 12:15 p.m."},{"start":218.4,"end":219.6,"text":"or from 1 p.m."},{"start":219.6,"end":220.96,"text":"to 5:15 p.m."},{"start":221.26,"end":227.34,"text":"A full day has morning and afternoon sessions, and children may eat at home or in the school dining room."},{"start":227.34,"end":232.46,"text":"These systems affect when families wake up, prepare food, travel, and arrange care."},{"start":233.2,"end":241.32,"text":"They also show different ways to organize responsibility: shared care of the school space in Japan and several timetable choices in Buenos Aires."},{"start":242.14,"end":244.24,"text":"Small rules can organize a whole day."},{"start":245.02,"end":252.66,"text":"In many Japanese homes, people take off their shoes at the entrance, creating a clear boundary between outside and inside space."},{"start":253.4,"end":263.26,"text":"In Bavaria, retail stores are generally closed on Sundays and public holidays, although petrol stations and shops in railway hubs can be legal exceptions."},{"start":264.1,"end":267.88,"text":"A family in Munich may therefore plan its food shopping before Sunday."},{"start":268.74,"end":273.14,"text":"In Argentina, mate is a daily drink and a social practice across the country."},{"start":273.64,"end":279.06,"text":"It can be drunk alone, but in a group one person prepares it and serves each person in turn."},{"start":279.48,"end":290.56,"text":"These habits seem unrelated, yet each one organizes shared life: the clean floor inside a home, the quiet rhythm of a Sunday, or a drink that gives people time to sit and talk."},{"start":291.56,"end":294.22,"text":"Imagine planning one Saturday in several places."},{"start":294.92,"end":299.84,"text":"In Tokyo, you may prepare for a quiet train journey and remove your shoes when entering a home."},{"start":300.62,"end":304.06,"text":"In Amsterdam, a bicycle may be the easiest choice."},{"start":304.98,"end":308.2,"text":"In Madrid, lunch can continue well into the afternoon."},{"start":308.86,"end":313.82,"text":"In Buenos Aires, you may load a SUBE card, meet friends for merienda, and share mate."},{"start":314.7,"end":318.28,"text":"In Munich, you may buy groceries before shops close for Sunday."},{"start":319.06,"end":327.28,"text":"The deeper lesson is that infrastructure, opening rules, meal schedules, and ideas about consideration make certain habits practical."},{"start":328.26,"end":330.18,"text":"This does not mean one routine is better."},{"start":330.58,"end":337.08,"text":"A system may save time in one place, support shared responsibility in another, or protect a quiet day."},{"start":337.84,"end":349.78,"text":"When a habit looks strange, ask what system or expectation makes this useful here; that question produces a more thoughtful comparison than simply asking what people from a country are like."}],"words":[{"text":"Europe","start":0,"end":0.28},{"text":"contains","start":